  "response": "Any assessment of the competing explanations begins with the size of the phenomenon to be explained. The national violent crime rate in 2000 was substantially lower than the rate in 1991; that is, violent crime in the United States fell substantially between 1991 and 2000. A drop of that magnitude, spread across regions, age groups, and offense types, sets a demanding standard. A credible explanation must predict the timing of the turn, must operate at a scale large enough to matter, and must survive comparisons between jurisdictions that adopted a given policy and those that did not. Policing strategies, incarceration, demographic change, legalized abortion, and reduced childhood lead exposure have each been tested against these criteria, with very different results.\n\nThe most influential attempt at a full accounting came from economics rather than sociology proper. Steven Levitt published \"Understanding Why Crime Fell in the 1990s\" in the Journal of Economic Perspectives in 2004. His procedure was to assemble published elasticity estimates for each candidate cause, multiply them by the observed change in that cause, and discard factors whose implied contribution was negligible or whose timing failed to match the decline. Within that framework, Levitt identified increased police numbers as one of four main factors behind the 1990s crime decline. The documentary support for that factor rests heavily on federal policy. Either the 1994 federal crime bill funded the hiring of additional police officers, which would confirm a genuine, policy-driven expansion of police numbers, or the bill provided no funding for the hiring of police officers; the two descriptions cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. Researchers have also used electoral cycles and grant timing to isolate staffing effects from the reverse tendency of high-crime cities to hire more officers.\n\nIncarceration has drawn the sharpest disagreement. Although Levitt estimated that rising incarceration accounted for roughly one third of the 1990s crime decline, a 2015 Brennan Center report estimated that increased incarceration accounted for a minor share of it. The gap arises largely from method. Levitt applied average effects drawn from the early part of the prison expansion, whereas the Brennan analysis modeled state-level variation across a longer period in which prison populations were already very large. The National Research Council committee resolved much of the dispute when it concluded that the growth in incarceration had a modest crime-reducing effect subject to diminishing returns. Under that reading, early additions to prison capacity removed high-rate offenders, and later additions removed progressively fewer. That finding indicates that increased incarceration reduced crime in the United States during the 1990s. Scholars wary of crediting prisons alone point to a second mechanism, and at least one of the two, perhaps both, operated: the incarceration effect, or the waning of the crack cocaine market, which reduced crime in the United States during the 1990s. By contrast with that drug-market account, and because of the Brennan report's small estimate for imprisonment, Levitt concluded that the strong 1990s economy was one of the four main factors behind the crime decline.\n\nPolicing strategy, as distinct from police numbers, raises its own difficulties of attribution. The New York Police Department introduced CompStat in 1994, whereas CompStat was first introduced by the Los Angeles Police Department in 1988. Isolating the effect of data-driven management is hard, because New York's decline began before the system was fully implemented and because cities without comparable reforms experienced similar drops. Some two decades after the New York introduction, the Brennan Center report concluded that CompStat-style policing contributed to the crime decline in the cities that adopted it.\n\nThe remaining explanations have fared unevenly. Demographic accounts founder on the modest size of the shift in the youth population during the decade. The legalized-abortion hypothesis remains contested over coding errors and cohort-specific patterns. Lead-exposure research, which links the phase-out of leaded gasoline to later cohorts' offending, has gained support from international comparisons but faces questions about ecological inference. The accounts that survive scrutiny are therefore those resting on credible identification strategies and plausible magnitudes, rather than those that merely coincide in time with the fall.",
